This is a common React performance issue! The problem is likely that you're creating a new object reference on every render, which causes React to think the props have changed even when the actual values are the same.

## Common Causes

### 1. **Creating objects inline in JSX**
```jsx
// ❌ Bad - creates new object on every render
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <Child 
      config={{ theme: 'dark', size: 'large' }} // New object every time!
      onClick={() => console.log('clicked')} // New function every time!
    />
  );
}
```

### 2. **Creating objects in render without memoization**
```jsx
// ❌ Bad - new object on every render
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const config = { theme: 'dark', size: 'large' }; // New object every time!

  return <Child config={config} />;
}
```

## Solutions

### 1. **Move static objects outside the component**
```jsx
// ✅ Good - object created once
const CONFIG = { theme: 'dark', size: 'large' };

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return <Child config={CONFIG} />;
}
```

### 2. **Use useMemo for dynamic objects**
```jsx
// ✅ Good - object only recreated when dependencies change
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('dark');

  const config = useMemo(() => ({
    theme,
    size: 'large'
  }), [theme]); // Only recreate when theme changes

  return <Child config={config} />;
}
```

### 3. **Use useCallback for functions**
```jsx
// ✅ Good - function only recreated when dependencies change
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    console.log('clicked');
  }, []); // No dependencies, so function never changes

  return <Child onClick={handleClick} />;
}
```

### 4. **Memoize the child component**
```jsx
// ✅ Good - Child only re-renders when props actually change
const Child = React.memo(({ config, onClick }) => {
  console.log('Child rendering');
  return <div>Child component</div>;
});
```

### 5. **Use state for complex objects**
```jsx
// ✅ Good - object reference stays stable
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [config] = useState({ theme: 'dark', size: 'large' });

  return <Child config={config} />;
}
```

## Complete Example

```jsx
import React, { useState, useMemo, useCallback } from 'react';

// Memoized child component
const Child = React.memo(({ config, onUpdate }) => {
  console.log('Child re-rendered');
  return (
    <div>
      <p>Theme: {config.theme}</p>
      <p>Size: {config.size}</p>
      <button onClick={onUpdate}>Update</button>
    </div>
  );
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('dark');

  // Memoize object prop
  const config = useMemo(() => ({
    theme,
    size: 'large'
  }), [theme]);

  // Memoize function prop
  const handleUpdate = useCallback(() => {
    setCount(c => c + 1);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>
        Increment (won't cause Child re-render)
      </button>
      <button onClick={() => setTheme(t => t === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark')}>
        Toggle Theme (will cause Child re-render)
      </button>
      <Child config={config} onUpdate={handleUpdate} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Quick Debug Tips

1. **Add console.logs** to see when components render
2. **Use React DevTools Profiler** to identify unnecessary re-renders
3. **Check if you're using React.memo** on child components
4. **Look for inline object/function creation** in JSX

The key is ensuring that object and function references remain stable between renders when their actual values haven't changed.

